  narrowest gap over the whole grid: 7.5e-3 (burn=20: floor 0.2359 vs market 0.2285)
  The burn rows narrow the gap only by CHANGING THE SAMPLE — a longer
  burn-in drops early-season games, which are exactly where a cold Elo is
  worst and the market's edge is largest; note the market's own Brier rises
  on those rows too. That is a smaller question, not a better model.
  No setting of any knob brings the public-statistics floor within reach
  of the venues, so the information-hierarchy claim is not an artifact of
  an under-tuned benchmark. The grid is scored on the same games the claim
  uses, so this is an UPPER bound on the floor's quality — a genuinely
  out-of-sample tuning could not do better.

